---js
{
  layout: "layouts/base.webc",
  meta: {
    title: "DynamoDB emulators",
    description: "Compare DynamoDB emulator support for features and API operations, with links to each target’s measured evidence.",
  },
  // Support is derived from the latest measured suite.
  eleventyComputed: {
    lastmod: (data) => data.conformance?.latest?.date,
  },
}
---
<script webc:setup>
  function projects(model) {
    return model.targets.filter(slug => {
      const row = model.perTarget[slug]?.current;
      return row && !row.baseline && slug !== 'dynamodb' && standsForProject(row);
    }).sort((a, b) => model.perTarget[a].display.localeCompare(model.perTarget[b].display));
  }
</script>
<section class="emulator-directory max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
  <p class="eyebrow">Project directory</p>
  <h1>DynamoDB emulators</h1>
  <p class="comparison-intro">What each emulator supports, what it needs to run, and where to get it. Feature support comes from the measured suite; installation information comes from project documentation.</p>
  <nav class="project-jumps" aria-label="Find an emulator"><a webc:for="slug of projects($data.conformance)" :href="'#project-' + slug" @text="$data.conformance.perTarget[slug].display"></a></nav>
  <details class="reading-guide"><summary>How to read feature support</summary>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">Supported means all feature tests pass. Partial means some pass while others fail or skip. Failing means the feature is implemented but no tests pass. Not supported means it is not implemented; not tested means there is no measurement. Each build is measured separately.</p>
  </details>
  <div class="project-directory" id="capabilities">
    <emulator-entry webc:for="slug of projects($data.conformance)" :target="$data.conformance.perTarget[slug]" :row="$data.conformance.perTarget[slug].current"></emulator-entry>
  </div>
  <suggest-target></suggest-target>
</section>
